# BloodLink app environment — copy to `.env` and adjust for your machine.
#
#   cp .env.example .env        (macOS / Linux)
#   copy .env.example .env      (Windows PowerShell)
#
# `.env` is gitignored: this file is per-developer.
#
# Metro inlines every EXPO_PUBLIC_* value at build time, so changing it requires
# restarting the dev server with a cleared cache:
#
#   npx expo start --clear
#
# Only non-secret values belong here — anything prefixed EXPO_PUBLIC_ is compiled
# into the shipped bundle and is readable by anyone with the app. That is exactly
# why the MongoDB connection string lives in backend/.env and never here: the app
# talks to the API, and the API holds the credentials.

# Where the app reaches the BloodLink API.
#
# Optional in development. Leave it unset and the app reuses the host Metro
# served this bundle from, swapping only the port (`services/api/client.ts`) —
# which is what makes a physical phone work without anyone having to know this
# machine's IP address. Set it explicitly for a real build, where Metro's host
# means nothing.
#
#   Android emulator, explicit -> http://10.0.2.2:4000
#   physical device,  explicit -> http://<this machine's LAN IPv4>:4000   (`ipconfig`)
#
# EXPO_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://localhost:4000
